In article <83083@brunix.UUCP> jg@cs.brown.edu (Jeremy Gaffney) writes:

   I agree wholeheartedly...I never use polearms as anything other than 
   missile weapons, despite the historic nature of the items.  I do vote
   that we replace them with other weapons, just more interesting ones-
   for instance, a lasso which could have a chance of binding an opponent,
   or leave the halberd in and have it attack from 2 spaces away,  like so:
   +------+
   |...*..|
   |..***.|	* = can hit with polearm
   |.**@**+
   |..***.|
   |...*..|
   +------+
   also, perhaps parrying weapons (main-gauche) which would do little damage
   but would increase the armor class...

Polearms are inappropriate for 1-on-1 battles; they are intended to be
used en masse.  Parrying weapons could work; you would have a small
chance of getting an extra attack each round, and you would get +1 AC,
though the main-gauche might break.

   In any case, it would also be interesting to have weapons which were
   not artifacts but would have non-plus kind of effects:  a Scimitar
   of Speed, a Dagger of Venom, an Axe of Stonecutting. Note that these
   would be items in and of themselves, not a random attribute so that 
   you might find (for instance) 33 -1 Darts of Stonecutting.  

What about special armor?  Insulated gloves, bouyant mail, gas mask,
+5 Magnesium Plate Mail :-), and shield of throwing!

   Heck, while we're tossing wish lists around,  let's throw in Boots 
   (slippers?) of Kicking, when worn, they'll add to your kicking potential.
   Other magic item ideas: Cursed wands  should have a chance to either shoot
   in a random direction or affect the wielder, and cursed rings of
   invisibility should start giving the  user paranoia:  glowing random colours
   like a ring of warning, occasional hallucination effects to make friendly or 
   tame monsters seem to change shape, and a gradual changing of alignment to
   Chaotic.  After all, we got Balrogs, we got Sting and Orcrist, we may
   as well have the one ring....>:)

And what happens if you give the ring to a hobbit?

I have a question that I asked David Hairston privately.  I would like
to know your thoughts.  Why are all the computer RPGs, including
Rogue, Hack, NetHack, Moria, Omega, DND, Ultima, Wizardry, etc. based
on D&D?  I'd like to see a computer RPG based on Runequest, or
Champions, or GURPS, or Bunnies and Burrows.  Well, maybe not B&B, but
you understand what I mean.  Any ideas?

